{
  "passage": "The Sundarbans lie in the delta formed by the Ganga, the Brahma-\nputra and the Meghna rivers, where they meet the Bay of Bengal. The\nregion is shared by India and Bangladesh, and it is the largest man-\ngrove forest in the world. Mangroves are trees and shrubs that can grow\nin salty water along the coast. Twice a day, the tides flood the forest\nfloor with sea water, and then the water goes back. Ordinary plants\ncannot survive in such salty, muddy soil, which has very little air.\nMangroves have special roots that solve this problem. Some of their\nroots grow upwards out of the mud, like pencils sticking out of the\nground. These breathing roots, called pneumatophores, take in air for the\nplant. The Sundarbans get their name from the sundari tree, one of the\nmost common mangroves there. The forest is the home of the Royal Bengal\ntiger, which has learnt to swim between the islands and to live in this\nsalty environment. Estuarine crocodiles, spotted deer and many kinds of\nfish and birds are also found there. The mangroves protect the coast.\nTheir thick roots hold the soil and break the force of waves during\ncyclones and storm surges, saving villages further inland. In 1987 the\nIndian part of the Sundarbans was declared a World Heritage Site by\nUNESCO.",
